    "abstract": "Accurate real-time train motion modeling is important for advanced railway control to increase line capacity and reduce energy use. However, key dynamic parameters in train motion modeling, including train mass and running resistance, are typically unavailable in real time, particularly for freight trains, where variability in customer needs and operational conditions results in significant uncertainty. In this paper, we propose a hybrid framework which jointly estimates these parameters from timestamped position records on coasting intervals without requiring additional dedicated onboard equipment for direct mass measurement. The framework consists of a Mass-Adaptive Unscented Kalman Filter module (MAUKF), together with a long short-term memory module (LSTM): the former reformulates the Davis equation to separate mass from running resistance and employs parameter-specific covariance adaptation for multi-scale parameters, while the latter refines the estimated parameters by learning structured residual patterns under a training loss that combines physics-based speed-rollout consistency, mass supervision, and MAUKF-based improvement terms. The proposed method is evaluated in a case study based on freight train data from the UK mainline network, measured on two major lines: Peterborough–Doncaster and Rugby–Crewe. Experimental results indicate that the proposed MAUKF–LSTM framework achieves typical final relative mass errors around 2% on the calibration route and below 1% on the unseen route in various conditions, while maintaining practicality for implementation in real time. These outcomes imply accurate mass estimation and physically plausible effective running-resistance estimation, which in turn supports increased line capacity and reduced energy consumption.",
